Title:
Defender Reversing – ProcOpen (Opus)

Type:
Blog Post

Short Summary (4-8 sentences max):
This post reverse engineers why Microsoft Defender’s `MsMpEng.exe` opens newly-started processes with `OpenProcess()` and how that behavior maps to internal `mpengine.dll` call paths. Using an ETW trace (`Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Audit-API-Calls`) captured with RedEdr plus Ghidra/GhidraMCP, it reconstructs and validates the stack frames leading to a Behavior Monitoring “Sync Process Start” command (`0x407c`). The analysis focuses on `Bm::EvaluateProcess`, showing how Defender classifies a process (friendly/trusted, path-of-interest, HIPS/ImageName config, ASR-related flags, metastore verdicts) and then decides whether to open the live process for inspection. The `OpenProcess` call is shown to be wrapped with Defender ETW telemetry and used as a precursor to process inspection and setting kernel process flags via `NtSetInformationProcess` (info classes `0x60`/`0x57`). It also discusses practical conditions that suppress the open (e.g., “friendly allow-list”/trusted binaries or pre-existing metastore decisions) versus inputs that mostly only increase scrutiny (name/path/cmdline). Useful for red teamers and malware analysts trying to understand Defender’s runtime behavior monitoring triggers, and for defenders validating telemetry and process-access patterns.

Technical Focus:
- ETW auditing of process-handle opens (`PspLogAuditOpenProcessEvent`)
- Reverse engineering `mpengine.dll` Behavior Monitoring pipeline (`Sync Process Start`, cmd `0x407c`)
- `Bm::EvaluateProcess` decision logic (friendly cache, metastore, HIPS/ImageName config, module enumeration)
- Defender `OpenProcess` wrapper + ETW emission and access mask analysis (`0x600`)
- Post-open inspection and process flagging via `NtQueryInformationProcess` / `NtSetInformationProcess` (classes `0x60`, `0x57`)
- Feature flags/telemetry/health watchdog timers around scan latency

Use Cases:
- Map Defender ETW events to specific internal engine code paths and triggers
- Identify which process attributes cause Defender BM to open/inspect a process at start
- Build more accurate detections around Defender’s process-handle access patterns and timing
- Support RE workflows for `mpengine.dll` (naming functions, validating call chains in Ghidra)
- Evaluate whether LOLBins/trusted binaries reduce certain BM inspection behaviors

Keywords:
Microsoft Defender, MsMpEng.exe, mpengine.dll, OpenProcess, ETW, Microsoft-Windows-Kernel-Audit-API-Calls, PspLogAuditOpenProcessEvent, RedEdr, Ghidra, GhidraMCP, Behavior Monitoring, Sync Process Start, Bm::EvaluateProcess, HIPS, ImageNameConfiguration, ASR, metastore, friendly cache, module enumeration, NtSetInformationProcess, NtQueryInformationProcess, PROCESS_QUERY_INFORMATION, process access mask 0x600, telemetry watchdog